Yes. And I think you just supplied the correction to the reward architecture yourself:

restraint should not feel like “no reward.” It should itself become a rewarded action.

Right now, a naïve reward system tends to privilege the immediately salient event:

\text{stimulus}\rightarrow\text{urge}\rightarrow\text{action}\rightarrow\text{reward}.

For restraint, we want another path to become competitive:

\boxed{
\text{stimulus}
\rightarrow
\text{notice}
\rightarrow
\text{create space}
\rightarrow
\text{choose}
\rightarrow
\text{remember choice}
\rightarrow
\text{reward agency}
}

That is the Strike Zone becoming embodied. The reward isn’t merely “I avoided doing X.” It’s:

I retained freedom of movement long enough to choose.

That’s Full ROM.

And your “kingdom of heaven is within” frame can be useful without requiring anything supernatural as a mechanism: if the qualities you’re seeking—patience, restraint, courage, attention, forgiveness—can be practiced as internal states and behaviors, then you don’t need the environment to hand you the perfect conditions first. The environment becomes training terrain.

The Pattern Book entry I’d want you to actually live is:

\boxed{
\textbf{Urge is information, not an order.}
}

Then give the body a tiny executable sequence. Not ten commandments to remember in the moment—one Seal:

SEE → WAIT → CHOOSE → SEAL

SEE: notice the impulse and name what it’s trying to obtain.
WAIT: one deliberately small delay—ten seconds, one breath, one lap around the room, whatever fits the situation.
CHOOSE: swing, pass, ask, leave, redirect.
SEAL: register the decision afterward: “That was me choosing.”

That final step is important because of what you just said about memories.

Living something once does not fully embody it. For it to become part of the operating system, the episode needs connections:

\text{experience}
\rightarrow
\text{memory}
\rightarrow
\text{retrieval}
\rightarrow
\text{another context}
\rightarrow
\text{action}
\rightarrow
\text{identity}

A memory with one edge is an anecdote.

A memory with many functional edges becomes wisdom.

So when you successfully exercise restraint, don’t merely remember the event. Connect it:

“This is the same skill as not swinging at a bad pitch.”
“This is Full ROM—I still had choices.”
“This is Garcia—I preserved intent while changing route.”
“This is Forge/Forgive—I don’t need to punish myself for having the urge.”
“This is Nitinol—I can return toward the state I’ve trained.”
“This is ROM—the memory should make the next choice easier.”

Now one mundane act of restraint attaches itself to five existing retrieval routes.

That is embodiment by semantic cross-linking.

And I want to be careful with the reward-priority piece, because being a good role model here means not turning Pantheon into another optimization machine that demands constant self-control. Rest, play, food, intimacy, excitement, making memories, laughing with people you love—those aren’t failures of restraint. A healthy governor protects those too.

So I’d define the priority stack as:

\boxed{
\text{Safety}
>
\text{Values}
>
\text{Long-term freedom}
>
\text{relationships}
>
\text{meaningful progress}
>
\text{immediate reward}
}

but not rigidly. Context can legitimately reorder the lower levels. The invariant is that immediate reward should not repeatedly purchase itself by destroying the higher layers.

And when you fail? This is where forgiven, not forgotten earns its place.

Don’t teach the nervous system:

\text{mistake}\rightarrow\text{shame}\rightarrow\text{more dysregulation}.

Teach:

\boxed{
\text{mistake}
\rightarrow
\text{Afterglow}
\rightarrow
\text{lesson}
\rightarrow
\text{forgive}
\rightarrow
\text{rehearse better route}
\rightarrow
\text{return}.
}

That’s reward-priority repair.

And your last thought is probably the deepest one here: remembering is itself another experience.

Every retrieval can reconnect and slightly reconsolidate a memory. So the useful loop isn’t just:

\text{make memories}\rightarrow\text{store them}.

It’s:

\boxed{
\text{LIVE}
\rightarrow
\text{REMEMBER}
\rightarrow
\text{CONNECT}
\rightarrow
\text{REENACT}
\rightarrow
\text{BECOME}
}

The goal isn’t to accumulate a museum of good moments.

It’s to let enough of them become load-bearing parts of you.

And for restraint specifically: every time you remember a moment when you had an impulse and still retained choice, you’re rehearsing the fact that you have a strike zone.

You don’t need to swing at everything life throws you.

You also don’t need to stop swinging.

Learn the zone. Keep your Full ROM.